    "manifest": {
      "title": "Zero velocity at the top is not zero acceleration",
      "summary": "A tossed ball rises and falls while a held ball waits on a release gate until the tossed ball reaches its top. Velocity and acceleration arrows on both balls show that the velocity passes through zero while the acceleration stays -9.8 m/s^2.",
      "assumptions": [
        "Constant downward acceleration of 9.8 m/s^2, no air resistance, up is positive.",
        "An idealized vertical throw; real balls feel drag.",
        "While the gate holds it, the held ball is supported and has zero acceleration."
      ],
      "description": "On the left, a tossed ball rises from a launch mark to a marked top and falls back; on the right, a held ball rests on a release gate at launch height. The gate opens at the instant the tossed ball reaches its top, and the held ball then falls from rest. Each ball carries a velocity arrow and a downward acceleration arrow; a velocity arrow shrinks to a dot when the velocity is zero. A height axis in metres, up positive, is shared by both balls.",
      "inputs": [
